    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.comment\n\n### Description\n\nSome users (me included) like to distinguish between explanatory/documenting comments, and actual code that was commented to avoid it being compiled/interpreted. Usually we use the padding before (and after if needed) the delimiter(s) to differ between those kinds of comments. In my case I use comments without padding to comments for inactivating code, and I use comments with padding to comments with explanatory nature.\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n--function commented_function() end\r\n-- This is a comment with explanatory nature.\r\n```\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n<!--# Commented Heading-->\r\n<!-- This is a comment with explanatory nature. -->\r\n```\r\n\r\nUsually users only toggle code or comments with actual code instead of explanatory/documenting code, since uncommenting explanatory comments is very likely to cause errors to the compiler/interpreter.\r\n\r\nWith that in mind, I think that would be very valuable to have an option to choose whether to use padding when commenting, uncommenting or toggling comments.",
